<!--
    Kuna pushan soundness: P2 flow-follow, option pushan (default auto).
    The script loads the synthetic static x86-64 ELF
    decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/pushan_vm2_x86_64
    (generator pushan_vm2_x86_64.py): one stack-machine interpreter in its own
    64 KiB executable section .vmp0 and twenty-six protected functions, each
    an entry stub `push key; jmp vm_entry` whose key selects a hand-written
    bytecode program whose decompilation is known.  Each program targets a
    way the devirtualizer could print something false about the program;
    twenty-five are decompiled here (f_arr's stack array, read back at a constant
    index after a loop fills it, is lost by the decompiler's own stack alias
    analysis, as it is in native code whose array pointer round-trips through
    the stack).

    f_sum (#1, #2): a counted loop, `for (i = 0; i < 10; i++) s += i`, whose
    exit test is a `setl` the virtual JZ tests and a cmov selects on; the
    loop exits and accumulates.
    f_eq (#3): a do-while whose exit test is an equality on the counter.
    f_big (#4): a loop bound of 2^40, beyond what the counter's known bits
    reach in the rounds it takes to converge.
    f_mem (#5, shared with f_corr) and f_corr (#6): a branch on a comparison
    stored through its address and reloaded, and two branches on the same
    comparison (both returns stay, as x + 10 and x + 20).
    f_call (#7): a value live across a native System V call is its argument.
    f_tbl (#8, #9): a read-only table indexed by the loop counter; the found
    path stays (table[2] is 300).
    f_glob (#10): a branch on a writable global is a test, not its initial 0.
    f_gcall (#11): a global the call writes is re-read after it.
    f_ptr (#12) and f_ploop (#13, #14): stores through a pointer argument on
    two paths, and a loop on a field behind it; the test after the join stays
    and the loop exits.
    f_join (#15): two virtual branches to one label keep both continuations.
    f_callp (#16): a local a call writes through its address is re-read.
    f_deep (#17) and f_ijmp (#18): a loop that grows the stack past the depths
    kept apart, and a virtual jump through an argument: the header says the
    program is incomplete (#19 counts both, f_spbit and f_ctxi) instead of
    staying silent.
    f_epi (#21, #22): both arms of a virtual branch leave the interpreter for
    the stub's native tail, `add rax, 1; mov rdi, rax; call fin; pop rbx;
    ret`, the else arm at the `add` and the then arm one instruction later.
    Both exits reach the tail with the native context, and the run that
    starts at the `add` ends where the other one starts, so `fin` is called
    once, on the value the two arms join with.
    f_sel (#23): x = a0 < 5 stored as 1 or 0 on two paths that join, then a
    virtual branch whose handler computes the next VPC arithmetically from
    the low bit of x and from c = a1 < 3 (VMProtect's conditional jump):
    the join merges x into a symbol seen as 0 and 1, which is no comparison,
    so the VPC is split over those two values and then over c, and all three
    returns stay with no unresolved jump.
    f_mba (#24): `return a0 + a1` through Themida-style handlers: each
    argument is read at `rbp + k` rebuilt as `(y & M) | (N - (~y & N))` plus
    `(rdx ^ rbx) + 2 * (rdx & rbx) - rdx - rbx`, a mixed boolean-arithmetic
    identity over the frame pointer and two more registers, and the exit
    compares the stack pointer with its own rebuilt form before returning,
    the other arm jumping to `fin` with the interpreter's frame still on the
    stack.  The identity test places both reads on the stack and decides the
    predicate, so the sum is returned, no call to `fin` appears (#21 still
    counts one) and the header stays complete (#19).
    f_spbit (#25): the exit tests bit 16 of the stack pointer, which no
    identity decides, and the other arm jumps to `fin` with the interpreter's
    frame still pushed: that path is no tail call, and the header says so
    instead of ending the function there in silence.
    f_out (#26, #27): v = 5 and x = a0[4], then ext7, a function of the
    image the exploration does not enter, gets &v as its seventh argument on
    the stack and a0 in RDI, and writes v and a0[4] (32 bytes past the
    pointer).  Both tests after the call stay: the call may write every frame
    slot at or above an address it was handed in any argument slot and every
    byte behind a pointer, so neither read is the value from before it.  The
    call prints its arguments, &v among them: RDI still holds the function's
    own first argument, which the emitter passes as the entry value it is.
    Taken for a register the program never set, it ended the argument list
    and the call printed as `ext7()`, so the C let no call change v.
    f_arg (#28): ext3(a0, 1, &v) with a0 left in RDI, then a test of v,
    which ext3 sets to 1.  The call prints a0, 1 and &v (and the address the
    interpreter left in RCX); before, `ext3()` with the test on a local no
    call could reach.
    f_below (#29): a loop whose call handler also copies the counter 0x100
    bytes below the stack pointer, in the callee's frame, and whose test
    after the call reads the counter.  The call overwrites that copy, so the
    rebuilt test reads the counter the loop increments; before, the
    exploration kept the copy across the call and the test read a frame
    slot nothing assigns.
    f_ctx (#30): a0 + 1 goes to a context cell inside .vmp0 (where Themida
    keeps its VM registers) and is read back at an offset the handler
    decodes as x ^ ((x & ~k) + (~x & k)) with x = rbx + c, which is k
    whatever rbx holds.  The identity test places that read on the cell, so
    it joins the cell's privatized register and the function returns
    a0 + 1.  Before, a constant over one non-pointer variable was left to
    three probe points that never accept it, the read stayed a load of the
    cell's memory, which the privatized store never reaches, and the
    function returned the cell's file contents.
    f_ctxi (#31): cells 1 and 2 = 1, 2, then the cell a0 & 1 selects is
    returned.  The exploration cannot place that read, but bounds it to the
    two cells, so they stay memory (their stores are kept), and the header
    says the body still reads and writes the protector's own memory.
    f_cell (#32): the interpreter copies its frame pointer to the unaligned
    context cell at vcell + 1, whose top byte shares an aligned word with the
    cell at vcell + 9, which the two arms of a branch set to a0 and to a1
    before they join; v = 1, then 2 is written to v through the copy and v
    is returned.  The merge symbolizes only the bytes the arms set
    differently, so the copy stays the frame pointer, the store lands on v
    and the function returns 2.  Before, the whole word was symbolized, the
    copy's top byte became a merge symbol, the store was placed nowhere and
    lost, and the function returned 1.
-->
